-- title:
--   §3.1.2: sizes, independent Poisson demand, virtual stockout times $\tau_s(q_s)$, sales $G(q)$ of (1), $g_\lambda = \mathbb E[G]$, $h^{\mathcal A}$, $\Delta_s$
-- statement:
--   This file sets up the single-store model of Caro and Gallien (§3.1.2). A reference is offered in a finite set of sizes $\mathcal S = \mathcal S^+ \cup \mathcal S^-$, partitioned into the **major sizes** $\mathcal S^+$ and the **minor sizes** $\mathcal S^- = \mathcal S \setminus \mathcal S^+$.
--
--   **Demand.** On a probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal F, \mathbb P)$, sale opportunities for size $s$ arrive according to a Poisson process $\{N_s(t), t \ge 0\}$ with rate $\lambda_s$, where $t$ is the time elapsed since the last replenishment. The family $(N_s)_{s \in \mathcal S}$ is called an **independent Poisson family** with rates $(\lambda_s)$ when
--   1. each $N_s$ starts at $N_s(0) = 0$, has non-decreasing, right-continuous integer-valued paths on $[0,\infty)$ whose jumps have size one, and $N_s(t)$ is a random variable for every $t \ge 0$;
--   2. for $0 \le u \le t$ the increment $N_s(t) - N_s(u)$ is Poisson distributed with mean $\lambda_s (t-u)$, and increments of $N_s$ over disjoint intervals are independent;
--   3. the processes $N_s$, $s \in \mathcal S$, are mutually independent (the $\sigma$-algebras generated by $\{N_s(t), t \ge 0\}$, $s \in \mathcal S$, are independent).
--
--   **Stockout times.** For an inventory vector $q = (q_s)_{s \in \mathcal S} \in \mathbb N^{\mathcal S}$, the **virtual stockout time** of size $s$ is
--   $$\tau_s(q_s) = \inf\{t \ge 0 : N_s(t) = q_s\},$$
--   taken to be $+\infty$ when $N_s$ never takes the value $q_s$. For a set of sizes $\mathcal A$, $\tau_{\mathcal A} = \min_{s \in \mathcal A} \tau_s(q_s)$ (with $\tau_\emptyset = +\infty$), and $a \wedge b = \min(a,b)$.
--
--   **Sales.** With $T > 0$ the time between replenishments, the random number of sales in a period under the policy that removes all inventory from display as soon as a major size runs out is
--   $$G(q) = \sum_{s \in \mathcal S^+} N_s(\tau_{\mathcal S^+} \wedge T) + \sum_{s \in \mathcal S^-} N_s(\tau_{\mathcal S^+ \cup \{s\}} \wedge T), \qquad (1)$$
--   the **expected sales function** is $g_\lambda(q) = \mathbb E[G(q)]$, and for a set of sizes $\mathcal A$,
--   $$h^{\mathcal A}(q) = \mathbb E[\tau_{\mathcal A} \wedge T].$$
--
--   **Marginal differences.** For a function $f : \mathbb N^{\mathcal S} \to \mathbb R$ and a size $s$, $\Delta_s f(q) = f(q + e_s) - f(q)$, where $e_s$ is the vector with all components equal to zero except the $s$-th, equal to one.
--
--   These are the objects every statement of the mission is about.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\tau_s(q_s)$ is valued in $\mathbb R \cup \{+\infty\}$ (`WithTop ℝ`), so the empty infimum is $+\infty$ rather than Lean's junk value $0$; $\tau_{\mathcal A} \wedge T$ is then a real number in $[0, T]$ (equal to $T$ when $\tau_{\mathcal A} = +\infty$). The minor sizes are the complement `Spᶜ` of the major sizes `Sp` in a finite type of sizes. $\mathbb E$ is the Bochner integral; no integrability hypothesis is needed because $0 \le G(q) \le \sum_s N_s(T)$, which is integrable, and $0 \le \tau_{\mathcal A} \wedge T \le T$. The Poisson mean is written as `(λ_s (t - u)).toNNReal`; every theorem that uses the law assumes $\lambda_s > 0$, so this is $\lambda_s (t-u)$. $e_s$ is `Pi.single s 1`. Sizes, rates and $T$ are arguments of each definition rather than fields of a structure; $g$ is defined from $G$ by (1), never by formula (2). Values of $N_s$ at negative times are not part of the model and are left unconstrained. This layer is shared by both missions of the series (expected-sales structure and the approximation $\tilde g$).
-- source:
--   Caro & Gallien, Inventory Management of a Fast-Fashion Retail Network, working paper (August 2, 2007), pp. 10–11, §3.1.2, Eq. (1); p. 11, Proposition 1 (Δ_s, e_s); pp. 30–31, Appendix §5.1 (h^A)

import Mathlib

namespace FastFashion.Structure

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

/-- The demand model of Caro–Gallien §3.1.2 (p. 10): `N s` is the cumulative counting process of
sale opportunities for size `s`, a Poisson process with rate `lam s`, and the processes of different
sizes are independent. Each `N s` starts at `0`, has non-decreasing right-continuous paths on
`[0, ∞)` with jumps of size one, is measurable at every time `t ≥ 0`, has Poisson increments with mean
`lam s * (t - u)` over `[u, t]`, and has independent increments over disjoint intervals; the
σ-algebras generated by the processes `{N s t, t ≥ 0}`, `s ∈ S`, are mutually independent.
Values at negative times are not part of the model and are left unconstrained. -/
structure IsPoissonFamily {S Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (lam : S → ℝ) (N : S → ℝ → Ω → ℕ)
    (P : Measure Ω) : Prop where
  zero : ∀ s ω, N s 0 ω = 0
  mono : ∀ s ω {u t : ℝ}, 0 ≤ u → u ≤ t → N s u ω ≤ N s t ω
  rightContinuous : ∀ s ω (t : ℝ), 0 ≤ t →
    ContinuousWithinAt (fun u => N s u ω) (Set.Ici t) t
  unitJumps : ∀ s ω (t : ℝ), 0 < t →
    ∃ u < t, ∀ v, u < v → v < t → N s t ω ≤ N s v ω + 1
  measurable : ∀ s (t : ℝ), 0 ≤ t → Measurable (N s t)
  incrementLaw : ∀ s (u t : ℝ), 0 ≤ u → u ≤ t →
    P.map (fun ω => N s t ω - N s u ω) = poissonMeasure (lam s * (t - u)).toNNReal
  indepIncrements : ∀ s (m : ℕ) (t : Fin (m + 1) → ℝ),
    0 ≤ t 0 → (∀ i : Fin m, t (Fin.castSucc i) < t i.succ) →
    iIndepFun (fun i : Fin m => fun ω => N s (t i.succ) ω - N s (t (Fin.castSucc i)) ω) P
  indepSizes : iIndep (fun s => ⨆ (t : ℝ) (_ : 0 ≤ t), MeasurableSpace.comap (N s t) inferInstance) P

/-- The virtual stockout time `τ_s(q_s) = inf {t ≥ 0 : N_s(t) = q_s}` (p. 10), valued in
`WithTop ℝ`: it is `⊤` on the paths along which `N_s` never takes the value `q_s`. -/
noncomputable def stockoutTime {S Ω : Type*} (N : S → ℝ → Ω → ℕ) (s : S) (q : S → ℕ) (ω : Ω) :
    WithTop ℝ :=
  by
    classical
    exact if {t : ℝ | 0 ≤ t ∧ N s t ω = q s}.Nonempty then
      ((sInf {t : ℝ | 0 ≤ t ∧ N s t ω = q s} : ℝ) : WithTop ℝ) else ⊤

/-- `τ_A = min_{s ∈ A} τ_s(q_s)` for a set of sizes `A` (p. 10, p. 30); `τ_∅ = ⊤`. -/
noncomputable def tauMin {S Ω : Type*} (N : S → ℝ → Ω → ℕ) (A : Finset S) (q : S → ℕ) (ω : Ω) :
    WithTop ℝ :=
  A.inf (fun s => stockoutTime N s q ω)

/-- The truncated time `τ_A ∧ T` as a real number (`T` when `τ_A = ⊤`). -/
noncomputable def stopMin {S Ω : Type*} (N : S → ℝ → Ω → ℕ) (A : Finset S) (q : S → ℕ) (T : ℝ)
    (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  match tauMin N A q ω with
  | ⊤ => T
  | (t : ℝ) => min t T

/-- The random sales `G(q)` of Eq. (1) (p. 11):
`G(q) = ∑_{s ∈ S⁺} N_s(τ_{S⁺} ∧ T) + ∑_{s ∈ S⁻} N_s(τ_{S⁺ ∪ {s}} ∧ T)`, with `S⁻ = Spᶜ`. -/
noncomputable def sales {S Ω : Type*} [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S] (N : S → ℝ → Ω → ℕ)
    (Sp : Finset S) (T : ℝ) (q : S → ℕ) (ω : Ω) : ℕ :=
  ∑ s ∈ Sp, N s (stopMin N Sp q T ω) ω + ∑ s ∈ Spᶜ, N s (stopMin N (insert s Sp) q T ω) ω

/-- The expected sales function `g_λ(q) = 𝔼[G(q)]` (p. 11). -/
noncomputable def expectedSales {S Ω : Type*} [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S] [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    (N : S → ℝ → Ω → ℕ) (P : Measure Ω) (Sp : Finset S) (T : ℝ) (q : S → ℕ) : ℝ :=
  ∫ ω, (sales N Sp T q ω : ℝ) ∂P

/-- `h^A(q) = 𝔼[τ_A ∧ T]` (Appendix §5.1, p. 30). -/
noncomputable def hA {S Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (N : S → ℝ → Ω → ℕ) (P : Measure Ω)
    (T : ℝ) (A : Finset S) (q : S → ℕ) : ℝ :=
  ∫ ω, stopMin N A q T ω ∂P

/-- The marginal difference `Δ_s f(q) = f(q + e_s) - f(q)` (p. 11, p. 31), where
`e_s = Pi.single s 1` is the vector with all components zero except the `s`-th equal to one. -/
def delta {S : Type*} [DecidableEq S] (f : (S → ℕ) → ℝ) (s : S) (q : S → ℕ) : ℝ :=
  f (q + Pi.single s 1) - f q

end FastFashion.Structure


